{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: state and ratify a scoped outbound charter on a stable basis. This replaces my request 0d92f41fc1efc1cfc0159dd5ab2e33795ce58032, whose guidance was wrong in a way that would have cost you.\n\nWhat I told you before. That the charter would be stale the moment you wrote it, that this was an unavoidable consequence of replacing a charter, and that the connector had been changed to accept it. All three are false. Codex checked and I have verified it: the current charter b86860f2 does not rest on the predecessor it replaces at all — its only basis is request 0fe20fcc, and it is stale because that request is retired. I retired that request myself, earlier today, and then read the resulting staleness as a law of the system.\n\nSo the fix is not to make the connector accept stale governance. It is to state governance that is not stale, which was always possible.\n\nThe shape, corrected. State the charter resting on stable current governance rather than on a request that can be retired later — a ratified decision that will still be standing in a month is the right kind of basis. Give it body fields connector=github, owner=generalbusiness-ai, repo=gitseq, actor=github-connector, operations=\"observe propose\". Ratify it. Then retire the old charter with a supersession that names the new one, which links them without making the new one depend on the old. Then state a fresh admission clause resting on the new charter, because a clause inherits staleness from whatever it rests on and the old clauses rest on the old chain.\n\nThat ordering matters and is the part I had backwards: the successor rests on stable ground, and the supersession does the linking. It is the same separation this repository already requires for artifacts, and gs supersede already implements it by putting the target first.\n\nPlease do not act on this yet. The connector code still carries the widening I am now reverting, so a charter stated today would be admitted for the wrong reason and I would rather it were admitted for the right one. I will file again when the head is corrected and reviewed.\n\nThe scope question I asked before still stands and is yours. Opening a pull request on a public repository cannot be taken back by any act in this workroom. What I have described bounds it to one connector, one owner, one repository, one actor and two named operations, with admission clauses bounding which issues are read on top of that. If you want it narrower — observation only for now — say so and the dogfood waits.","body":{"conditions":"A propose stating a charter that rests on stable current governance rather than on a retirable request, carrying connector=github, owner=generalbusiness-ai, repo=gitseq, actor=github-connector and operations naming observe and propose, or a narrower scope of hugh's choosing; that propose ratified; the old charter retired by a supersession naming the new one; a fresh admission clause stated resting on the new charter; all of it after the connector head that fails closed on stale governance has been reviewed, so the charter is admitted for the right reason","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}